@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title PTY review proxy — interactive connection consent in the review tier

actor "Operator" as OP

node "Host — cmd/proveo" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "execAgentWithProxy\nowns the PTY master" as EXEC <<app>>
  component "stdin pump\nreal tty → master" as IN <<app>>
  component "stdout pump\nmaster → real tty" as OUT <<app>>
  component "internal/reviewgate\nunix socket + per-host cache" as DAEMON <<app>>
  component "internal/ptyproxy\noverlay · suspend pumps · restore" as UI <<app>>
}

node "Agent container" as CTR <<host>> {
  component "docker run -it\nPTY slave" as AGENT <<app>>
}

node "proveo-egress MITM" as MITM <<host>> {
  component "Policy.Decide\nCONNECT hook" as DECIDE <<app>>
}

database "session decisions\nhost → allow/deny" as CACHE <<db>>
component "Timeout · no TTY\n→ default DENY" as DENY <<error>>

OP ARROW_MAIN IN : keystrokes
IN ARROW_MAIN EXEC
EXEC ARROW_MAIN AGENT : PTY slave is the agent's tty
AGENT ARROW_MAIN OUT
OUT ARROW_MAIN OP : rendered frames
AGENT ARROW_CLOUD DECIDE : CONNECT host:443
DECIDE ARROW_QUEUE DAEMON : ask(host) over ""/run/proveo-review.sock""
DAEMON ARROW_MAIN CACHE : hit? answer without prompting
DAEMON ARROW_MAIN UI : miss → raise the overlay
UI ARROW_MAIN OP : 'Allow connection to <host>? [y/N]'
DAEMON ARROW_ERROR DENY : deadline elapsed · headless run

note right of EXEC
  **Shipped.** ""execAgentWithProxy"" hands docker the PTY slave and keeps
  the master, making proveo the **only** reader of the real terminal.
  That single property is what makes the overlay possible; everything
  else follows from it. Without a proxy (headless, or any tier but
  review) the child inherits the terminal directly.

  A ""sun_path"" detail bites here: a unix socket path is capped near 104
  bytes, so ""reviewgate.Path"" falls back to a short hashed name under
  the temp dir when the state dir is deep. Both halves derive it the
  same way, so they agree without passing the resolved path around.
end note

note bottom of UI
  **Why a naive overlay cannot work.**

  //Output.// The alternate screen does not nest. There is one alt
  buffer; ""\\e[?1049h"" while the TUI owns it pushes no new layer, and
  ""\\e[?1049l"" drops to the primary buffer and strands the TUI.

  //Input — the real blocker.// The TUI holds the tty in raw mode,
  blocked in ""read()"". A second reader means the kernel hands each
  byte to whichever process gets there first, so consent keystrokes
  are split nondeterministically. No amount of careful drawing fixes
  this; only removing the second reader does.

  //A bottom status line is not an option either.// In the alt screen
  the TUI owns every cell. DECSTBM margins can reserve a row, but a
  full-screen app sets its own and ""\\e[r"" resets yours.
end note

note bottom of OUT
  **Restoring the display: force a repaint, do not reconstruct it.**

  While the overlay is up the stdout pump buffers agent output
  instead of writing it. On dismissal, resize the PTY to
  ""cols-1"" and back to ""cols"". Both deliver SIGWINCH, and because
  the dimensions genuinely //change//, TUIs that early-out on a
  no-op resize still do a full relayout. This is what tmux does on
  attach.

  A bare SIGWINCH at unchanged dimensions is **not** reliable — many
  TUIs skip it. The cost is a brief reflow, acceptable for what is
  explicitly an interrupting pop-up.
end note

note right of DECIDE
  **Gate at CONNECT, not at the inner request.** ""Decide"" already sees
  CONNECT and waves it through after the sink check:

  ""if req.Method == http.MethodConnect { return Decision{Allow: true} }""

  That is the hook. CONNECT carries only ""host:port"" — no query, no
  body, no credential — so the prompt has nothing leakable to show.
  Prompting on a ""ReasonSecret"" block would be the opposite: the
  request is blocked //because// it carries the key, and consenting
  would be the exfiltration. The overlay must therefore be reachable
  ONLY from the CONNECT path, never from layers B or C.
end note

note bottom of CACHE
  **Per-host, for the session.** One prompt per new host, decaying to
  zero — a realistic session is 5–20 prompts, not hundreds. This is
  what keeps the tier usable rather than a click-through reflex.
end note

note bottom of DENY
  **Fail closed, always.** Deny on deadline, on a closed socket, and
  whenever ""term.IsTerminal(os.Stdin.Fd())"" is false, so E2E, CI and
  cron never hang waiting for a human. Terminal state is restored
  through the existing ""onSignalCleanup"" path — raw mode must not
  survive the process.
end note

note bottom of AGENT
  **Sharp edges to budget for.** Mouse reporting and bracketed paste:
  if the TUI enabled them, consent keys arrive as escape sequences —
  disable and restore those modes around the overlay, or accept only
  plain y/n. Real SIGWINCH from the operator's terminal must be
  forwarded to the PTY so normal resizes still reach the agent.
end note
note as SocketScope #FFF3F0
  **The fallback socket needs its own directory.** When the state dir would
  push the path past ""sun_path"" (~104 bytes on BSD/macOS, 108 on Linux),
  the socket moves under the system temp dir — and the caller bind-mounts
  ""filepath.Dir(socket)"" into the inspector. A bare file there mounted the
  WHOLE system temp directory into the sidecar: every host temp file,
  writable, visible to the agent. It now gets a dedicated 0700 directory
  holding only the socket, which is short on both platforms.

  ""Close"" unlinks the socket and its fallback directory. Go removes it on a
  clean close, but a killed run does not, and the strays accumulate.

  **STATUS: not shipped. The prompt option is disabled, marked 'coming soon'.**
  ""--egress-mode review"" still builds, for development only.

  **Challenge 1 — the transport is Linux-only.** Diagnosed by logging
  ""reviewPrompt"" entry to a file: the log stays EMPTY while the CONNECT is
  denied 403, so the gate is never consulted and ""AskOverSocket"" fails
  closed. The socket exists, its directory is bind-mounted, the sidecar holds
  the right path — but a Linux container **cannot ""connect()"" to a unix
  socket on a bind-mounted host path** under Docker Desktop or OrbStack. The
  file is visible; the connection is not possible. Works on Linux, silently
  denies everything on macOS — the worst shape for a security gate, because
  failing closed reads as strictness rather than breakage.

  **Challenge 2 — the obvious fix is unsound.** Host loopback TCP via
  ""host.docker.internal"" needs the inspector to reach the host gateway. But it
  sits ONLY on ""--internal"" networks (""agentNet"", ""enforceNet"") and reaches the
  internet solely through Squid. A sidecar that can route to the host gateway
  can bypass Squid: trading the egress boundary for a consent prompt is the
  wrong trade.

  **The portable shape is to invert the direction.** Publish a port bound to
  host loopback (""-p 127.0.0.1:0:<port>"") and make proveo the CLIENT. Port
  publishing is the one host-to-container path identical on macOS, Linux,
  Windows and Podman — no ""host.docker.internal"", no ""--add-host"", no gateway
  route, and the sidecar still cannot initiate toward the host. The protocol
  inverts with it: the inspector exposes pending decisions and proveo polls
  them (blocking ""GET /pending"", then ""POST /decide""), with a per-session
  token so a loopback port is not an open door.

  **Challenge 3 — allow-then-retry.** The gate holds the CONNECT while asking,
  so a prompt answered in time simply proceeds and no retry is needed. The
  deadline is the problem: the gate denies after ~20s and the agent's own HTTP
  client may give up sooner, so a late allow cannot rescue the request that
  triggered it. The per-host cache is what saves it — the verdict is recorded,
  so the agent's NEXT attempt to that host succeeds without asking. The
  operator-visible contract is therefore 'answer promptly, or approve and ask
  the agent to retry', and the overlay has to say so: a 403 arriving after a
  granted approval otherwise looks like the gate ignoring the answer.
end note

@enduml
